************** PLEASE READ ME *********************

A PrivateString is intended to be used as a field reference for
BasisString/VolatileString. To get a VolatileString or BasisString from a
field, call `BasisString#asVolatileString(ConstString)` or
`BasisString#asBasisString(ConstString)`. To set the value of this
private string, use `#setValue(ConstString)`.

DO NOT PASS REFERENCES to an instance of this class around. This class is
named "private" because it's intended to stay private. If you need to pass a
temporary reference, pass a `VolatileString`. It should be rare to need
to pass a `BasisString`. It's always safer, but may be inefficient to
do so. Converting to a `BasisString` always increments the buffer's
refcount, while converting to a `VolatileString` never increments the
buffer's refcount.

It's also good practice to make fields of type `PrivateString`
`final`. Rather than assign to the PrivateString field, use
`PrivateString#setValue(ConstString)` and
`PrivateString#clearValue()`.

The expected typical execution path is something like this:

public class Foo {
private final PrivateString x = BasisString.makePlatformPrivateString("Foo");
...
public VolatileString foo() {
...
return BasisString.asVolatileString(x);
}
}

public class Bar {
private final PrivateString y = BasisString.makePrivateString();
...

public void bar(VolatileString p_value) {
y.setValue(p_value);
}
}

public class Baz {
public static void main(String[] argv) {
Foo f = new Foo();
Bar b = new Bar();
b.bar(f.foo());
}
}

As long as no changes are made to the buffer between these two points, using
a VolatileString is safe. It's expected that mutating the buffer will be very
rare, or will generally occur on new BasisString values.

`PrivateString` is somewhat special in that it can represent a 'null'
buffer value. Many of the methods that would normally operate on a buffer
will thus throw a NullPointerException if an instance of
`PrivateString` represents a `null` byte string. Some notable
exceptions include `#hashCode()`, `#equals(Object)` and
`#toString()`. The exact behavior is documented in each of those
methods, but the rationale is that since many of the places these methods
might be used are to already have `null` checks and because those
`null` checks would fail for non-null `PrivateString` references,
these methods will not not throw `NullPointerException`, and will
instead exhibit `null`-compatible behavior.

methods.docu:
Use this method like this:

catch (SomeException e)
{
throw Util.initCause(new OtherException("Error occurred"), e);
}

**Parameters:**

- `p_thrown` The object to throw.
- `p_cause` The Throwable argument to pass to initCause().

**Returns:** This method always throws `p_thrown`, so it never returns
normally.

classes.docu:
Interface which will automatically be wrapped in an autoproxy
when created by FacadeFactory.
All AutoWrappables should contain the following code. In addition,
All AutoWrappables should call getAutoProxy in their constructor

NOTE: include this code in all AutoWrappables:

private WeakReference m_autoProxyRef;
private Integer m_parentProcessThreadID;

public Object getAutoProxy()
{
Object ret;
if(m_autoProxyRef == null)
{
Object autoProxy = BaseInvocationHandler.createAutoProxy(this);
m_autoProxyRef = new WeakReference(autoProxy);
}
getParentProcessThreadID();
return m_autoProxyRef.get();
}

public Object getAutoProxy(com.basis.bbj.util.AutoWrappable p_parent)
{
Object ret = getAutoProxy();
m_parentProcessThreadID = p_parent.getParentProcessThreadID();
return ret;
}

public Integer getParentProcessThreadID()
{
if(m_parentProcessThreadID == null)
m_parentProcessThreadID = ThreadID.getID();
return m_parentProcessThreadID;
}
